 are major agents of change working on the surface of the earth.", "conf": 0.91, "box": [[91, 871], [869, 854], [869, 881], [92, 897]]}, {"text": "These agents have developed following types of plains through erosional and depositional", "conf": 0.938, "box": [[90, 898], [871, 882], [871, 909], [91, 925]]}, {"text": "processes. Erosion is the wearing away and removal of exposed land by river, wind and glacier.", "conf": 0.932, "box": [[90, 926], [870, 910], [870, 936], [90, 953]]}, {"text": "Depositional process is the accumulation of load carried bythese agents.", "conf": 0.908, "box": [[90, 955], [670, 943], [671, 967], [90, 978]]}, {"text": "i. Erosional Plains: When a mountain or plateau area is reduced to a low lying plain by", "conf": 0.918, "box": [[88, 990], [869, 974], [869, 1000], [88, 1016]]}, {"text": "weathering and erosion over a very long period of time, an erosional plain is formed which is", "conf": 0.943, "box": [[86, 1017], [871, 1001], [871, 1028], [86, 1045]]}, {"text": "termedas peneplain. The plains of Northern Europe and Northern Canada are erosional plains.", "conf": 0.907, "box": [[87, 1046], [855, 1030], [855, 1055], [87, 1070]]}, {"text": "i. Depositional Plains: These are formed by the deposition of sediments on lowlands, brought", "conf": 0.918, "box": [[86, 1082], [868, 1066], [868, 1092], [86, 1108]]}, {"text": "by rivers, glaciers or the wind.", "conf": 0.919, "box": [[85, 1109], [324, 1106], [324, 1129], [85, 1133]]}, {"text": "Unit 1:Plains and Rivers", "conf": 0.889, "box": [[81, 1166], [238, 1165], [238, 1185], [81, 1186]]}]}